ds are closed just being able to get  into some of the  neighborhood is the difficulty do you  have any update on casualties I don't at  this time it's been miraculous that  there haven't been more the early  casualties one is too many but appears  to be a handful and most of them were  related to being in vehicles and driving  into high water which is why we didn't  want people on the roads as if they then  unfolded scale you understand some of  the injuries that you're dealing with I  saw a very touching piece this morning  where someone was helping a woman rescue  from the water and he said listen if the  water is very cold but don't worry we're  going to be here to help you don't worry  about that and I hadn't really thought  about the temperature of the water what  kind of injuries are you dealing with  we're seeing well of course anytime  people are trying to well let me back up  first we encourage people to stay in  their home if at all possible because  going out into the flood water is  dangerous debris is in it manhole covers  come off and you could fall in you don't  know what the terrain is under your your  footing and so there are injuries that  are just going to happen but right now  you know my concern is these first  responders have been doing this now for  two days without rest people are really  beginning to get worn out and whenever  you get that tired then it makes it even  easier to injure yourself any idea  what's in the water and you know if your  body stays in contact with it for too  long and if you ingest it what do you  tell people when it comes to making that  decision do I go or do I stay well we  always say if at all possible stay in  your in your home if the water such that  your life is threatened and of course we  state down 9-1-1 but the 9-1-1 system we  have three major 9-1-1 call centers city  of Houston and Harris County and they  were overwhelmed with people calling for  help I had people calling me directly  who happened to know my cell phone and  they'd say listen there's a woman who  has gone into labor and such  such place can you help get her out we  have 4.7 million people living in Harris  County and of course the surrounding  counties the overall region at about six  and a half million so it's just a  massive massive dire situation you make  a very good point about the work that  the first responders have been doing and  how tired they must be and that's why  you're calling on volunteers to help a  man was watching the news he said listen  I was sitting home watching you guys and  I thought let me get out there and help  I'm an able-bodied man  so how critical are the volunteers to  this effort absolutely critical and and  not only those who can come out and help  with the rescue we're sending up so many  shelters people can go fallen tear in  those shelters to serve food to provide  supplies and interestingly enough the  schools are all closed for the entire  week so you know the kids that are old  enough take them let them help too this  is something that neighbor to neighbor  just we just have to pitch in and help  each other okay thank you very much  judge Edie Emmet there Harris County top  official when it comes to public safety  we appreciate the time good luck to you  thank you thank you
 

